PART 2 — THE PROBLEM WE ARE SOLVING

Alva already has several good combat mechanics.

Players can rapidly transition between different attacks, cancel an attack with another valid action, buffer slightly early inputs, and use Combat Assist to close small gaps.

The problem is that repeating the same attack button while its animation is playing is currently discarded.

Consider the following example.

A beginner presses Attack 1.

Attack 1 begins.

The beginner presses Attack 1 again because they want to continue fighting.

Nothing happens.

This is intentional in the current implementation, because restarting the same animation would create serious problems.

However, completely discarding the input can also make combat feel unresponsive or confusing.

The player believes they requested another attack, but the game gives no follow-up.

Our solution must address that problem without sacrificing the protection provided by the existing rule.

2.1. Why simply allowing the second press is wrong

A naïve implementation of Attack 1 → Attack 1 could restart the animation.

That could:

* Cancel Attack 1 before its hitbox becomes active.
* Restart its startup repeatedly.
* Prevent the existing animation from completing.
* Cause inconsistent hit registration.
* Create unintended loops.
* Interfere with the cooldown policy.
* Make animation timing difficult to understand.
* Allow abusive button mashing if other safeguards fail.

We absolutely do not want this.

The currently playing attack must be protected from a repeated press.

We want the second press to be remembered as a future follow-up request, not executed immediately.

