PART 4 — NATURAL COMPLETION VS INTERRUPTION

This is a critical engineering requirement.

Alva’s current combat lifecycle can end an attack for several different reasons.

Those reasons are not interchangeable.

4.1. Natural completion

An attack ends because its own timeline reaches the full duration defined by its animation and phases.

A queued follow-up may execute after this kind of completion.

4.2. Manual cancellation

A player intentionally replaces the attack with a different valid action.

The original attack is interrupted.

Its existing cooldown begins.

Any queued Combo Assist follow-up attached to the original attack must be discarded.

Do not trigger that queued follow-up later.

4.3. Hit interruption

A fighter takes a hit that interrupts its current action.

Any pending queued Combo Assist follow-up must be discarded.

The queue must not revive after hitstun, paralysis, or recovery.

4.4. Landing cancellation

Some aerial attacks end their recovery early when landing.

This is not equivalent to their complete animation naturally finishing.

Do not release an unfinished queued attack because a landing cancel ended the source.

4.5. Other forced endings

Clear stale queues when their source attack ends because of:

* A successful Dash or air dash cancel.
* A successful jump or air jump cancel.
* Shield or Deflect taking over.
* A different valid combat action taking over.
* A technique or summon beginning.
* Knockback or other forced combat states.
* The fighter leaving the active match.
* Falling into the Void.
* Respawning or resetting.
* Changing fighters.
* Starting a new round or rematch.
* Battle or practice-session destruction.
* Input locking or a completed match.
* Any other existing transition that makes the original attack obsolete.

Do not introduce a single broad clearing rule that accidentally destroys legitimate input buffers.

Distinguish the new Combo Assist queue from the existing ordinary attack buffer.

4.6. Implementation requirement

Study the exact behavior of:

* CombatState.update()
* CombatState.endAttack()
* CombatState.interruptAttack()
* Fighter.cutAttack()
* Fighter.update()
* Fighter.startAttack()
* Landing cancellation
* Hit processing

Choose a robust mechanism for identifying a genuine natural completion.

Possible approaches include tracking a completion event or explicitly recording the reason an attack ended.

Choose the smallest reliable implementation compatible with the current architecture.

Do not use the weak assumption that combat.attack === null always means the animation finished.

Do not use elapsed attack time alone if another action could have interrupted the move at the same boundary.

Ensure the source attack’s entire intended animation, including the final frame’s intended display interval, is preserved.

The queued follow-up should start on the earliest legal simulation step after that complete natural ending, without an arbitrary additional delay.

