PART 6 — KEEP NORMAL INPUT BUFFERING SEPARATE

Alva already provides an ordinary attack input buffer of 0.15 seconds.

This buffer remembers a legitimate attack request that was made slightly before it became executable.

The proposed Combo Assist queue serves a different purpose.

Existing input buffer

An attack was requested while temporarily unavailable.

The request may be retried for the existing buffer duration.

New Combo Assist queue

A repeated basic attack requests its alternate, specifically after the currently playing attack completes naturally.

These are distinct systems.

Do not combine their timers or make one silently extend the other.

6.1. Separation of state

Use a dedicated, clearly named queue field or equivalent isolated state.

A suggested conceptual structure is:

queuedCombo = {
  sourceRecord,
  sourceAttackId,
  requestedAction,
  originatingButton,
  originatingPlace,
  createdStep,
}

Adapt the structure to the actual codebase.

Preserve the reference or identity of the precise source attack instance, not merely its attack ID.

That prevents a queued request from leaking into a later instance of the same move.

6.2. Newer requests and priority

Preserve the current precedence of genuine player input.

A queued attack is a fallback request, not an action that overrides the player.

If the player deliberately starts another valid action before the original attack completes, the newer action should take priority and the old queue should be removed.

A new queued follow-up must not steal the step from a successful fresh input.

Do not let a failed or unavailable action randomly cancel a legitimate queue unless the game’s established input-priority rules explicitly require it.

An action pressed simultaneously with the natural completion of an attack must be resolved consistently.

Guarantee that at most one new move can be accepted in a simulation step.

The design must avoid duplicate attack starts caused by the same press being interpreted through both ordinary Combo Assist and the new queue.

6.3. Never create ghost inputs

A ghost input is a combat action that activates later when the player reasonably believes the request is gone.

The new queue must not produce this behavior.

It should be bound to one specific active attack and its natural end.

After the current attack is canceled or the queue is consumed, the old request must be permanently cleared.

Do not let it survive unrelated actions.

