PART 8 — COMBAT ASSIST INTEGRATION

Alva has a separate feature named Combat Assist.

Combat Assist helps the human player’s melee attacks connect by closing a short, valid gap before executing the requested attack.

It is independent of Combo Assist.

Both systems can be enabled or disabled separately.

Preserve that independence.

8.1. Queued melee attacks

Suppose Attack 1 finishes and Attack 2 is queued.

If Attack 2 is a melee attack and Combat Assist is enabled:

1. Check that Attack 2 can legally start.
2. Evaluate its ordinary Combat Assist requirements.
3. If the opponent is already within range, execute it normally.
4. If Combat Assist can legally close the gap, begin the existing approach.
5. Execute Attack 2 after the approach finishes, through its standard pathway.

Do not teleport the fighter.

Do not extend the attack’s hitbox.

Do not increase its damage or tracking strength.

Do not bypass stage collision.

Do not change Combat Assist’s existing Energy behavior.

8.2. Both assists disabled or enabled independently

Test all four combinations:

Combo Assist	Combat Assist	Expected behavior
On	On	Queued alternate plus normal melee approach eligibility
On	Off	Queued alternate without assisted approach
Off	On	Existing Combat Assist; no new combo queue
Off	Off	Existing manual combat behavior

Do not couple the two settings.

8.3. Preserve facing

Combat Assist currently contains special handling to face the opponent correctly, including while Run is held.

Do not regress that behavior.

A queued follow-up must use the proper attack-start facing and current movement context.

It must not aim in the wrong direction simply because the player is holding Run away from the opponent.

Likewise, do not override deliberate manual facing when Combat Assist is disabled.

