PART 13 — ANIMATION INTEGRITY

One of the most important outcomes of this task is that repeated button presses must stop feeling destructive to animations.

The implementation must guarantee:

1. A same-move repeat never restarts the source attack.
2. A queued follow-up never cancels the source attack.
3. Source attack animation time advances naturally, including startup, active and recovery.
4. Valid multi-hit strikes are not accidentally skipped.
5. A queued request does not erase a pending projectile release.
6. Previously released projectiles remain governed by normal rules.
7. An attack’s visual animation and gameplay timeline remain synchronized.
8. The follow-up begins from its own first frame.
9. The final frame of the source animation is not accidentally skipped.
10. An interrupted attack never masquerades as a naturally completed one.

Study SpriteAnimator and Fighter.updateState() before implementing the handoff.

Do not solve the problem by directly forcing an animator restart or adjusting its frame index.

The proper solution is combat-state-controlled sequencing.

