PART 14 — HITSTOP, STUN AND FRAME-BOUNDARY EDGE CASES

The implementation must be reliable at exact simulation boundaries.

14.1. Hitstop

Hitstop freezes the relevant combat motion but does not necessarily stop the player from pressing buttons.

If a legitimate repeat press occurs during hitstop while the source attack remains active, handle it consistently with the queue rules.

Do not count frozen animation time as completed time.

Do not allow the queued attack to execute while the fighter remains frozen.

If a hit interrupts the source attack, discard the queue.

Test attacks that hit, miss, and are blocked.

14.2. Final-frame input

Pay particular attention to a repeat press on the exact step when an attack reaches its ending boundary.

Depending on the current update order, CombatState.update() may finish the attack before the player’s press is resolved.

Ensure the press is handled once, not lost because of an inconsistent boundary interpretation and not executed twice.

The final-frame behavior must match the intended Combo Assist semantics.

14.3. Same-step competing actions

Test a queued follow-up against simultaneous requests involving:

* Another attack button.
* Jump.
* Dash.
* Shield.
* Deflect.
* A special attack.

Preserve the current combat-input priority.

One step must never launch two ordinary attacks because of a queue race.

14.4. Interrupted queues

Test source attacks interrupted during:

* Startup.
* Active frames.
* Recovery.
* Hitstop.
* The last eligible queue frame.

No ghost follow-up should execute.

