FINAL DESIGN PHILOSOPHY

Alva should be easy to begin playing but difficult to master.

A beginner should be able to press a basic attack button, press it again at the appropriate moment, and understand that the game has accepted a follow-up.

An experienced player should still be able to use precise move selection, manual cancels, spacing, shielding, aerial movement and timing to perform better.

Combat Assist helps the player reach the opponent.

Combo Assist helps the player select and sequence basic attacks.

Input buffering helps the game recognize slightly early inputs.

Cooldowns preserve each move’s reuse limits.

Manual cancellation preserves the depth of high-skill combat.

These systems should cooperate without overriding each other.

The game should never feel unresponsive simply because an input was discarded unnecessarily.

But responsiveness must not come from restarting animations, ignoring cooldowns, producing unintended attacks, or changing CPU intelligence.

The ideal result is a combat experience that feels:

Responsive. Fluid. Predictable. Accessible. Skillful. Fair. Visually coherent.

Implement the improvement carefully, validate it thoroughly, and leave Alva’s existing combat architecture stronger and easier to maintain than before.

Begin by inspecting the latest repository, then implement and test the solution.
